## 7. Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD)

Slowly Changing Dimensions define how changes to dimension attributes are recorded.

### SCD Type 0 — Retain the Original Value

The original value remains unchanged.

Use this approach when the first recorded value must be preserved.

### SCD Type 1 — Overwrite

The old value is replaced with the new value.

Historical values are not preserved in the dimension row.

Example: Correcting a misspelled customer name.

### SCD Type 2 — Preserve History

A new dimension row is created whenever a tracked attribute changes.

Common columns include:

- Business key
- Surrogate key
- Effective start date
- Effective end date
- Current-row indicator

Example:

| Customer Key | Customer ID | City | Start Date | End Date | Current |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 101 | C001 | Cairo | 2024-01-01 | 2025-05-31 | No |
| 205 | C001 | Alexandria | 2025-06-01 | NULL | Yes |

This allows historical reports to reflect the attributes that were valid at the time of a transaction.

### SCD Type 3 — Limited Previous Values

The current value and one or more previous values are stored in separate columns.

This preserves limited history rather than a full sequence of changes.

### Choosing an SCD Type

- Type 0: Never change the recorded value.
- Type 1: Replace the value without retaining history.
- Type 2: Preserve historical versions in separate rows.
- Type 3: Preserve limited history in separate columns.

