## 14. Medallion Architecture

Medallion architecture organizes data into layers with increasing refinement.

### Bronze Layer

Purpose: Preserve source data in raw or minimally changed form.

Common practices:

- Retain source records
- Add ingestion metadata
- Track batch or run identifiers
- Support replay and reprocessing
- Preserve traceability

Schema handling depends on the platform and design; Bronze does not universally mean that no schema exists.

### Silver Layer

Purpose: Clean and standardize data.

Typical operations:

- Data validation
- Deduplication
- Data type normalization
- Standardization
- Business-rule application
- Joining related datasets
- Quality checks
- Controlled schema handling

### Gold Layer

Purpose: Prepare business-ready data.

Examples:

- Fact tables
- Dimension tables
- Aggregated metrics
- KPIs
- Curated data products
- Reporting datasets

The medallion pattern is useful but not mandatory. Some architectures combine layers or use different names.

